    "['ipv6 pim [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols pim interface <interface-name> family inet6 mode sparse', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent command for `ipv6 pim sparse-mode`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols pim interface <interface-name> family inet6 mode sparse\\n##\\n\\nNote: You would replace `<interface-name>` with the actual Juniper interface name (e.g., `ge-0/0/0`, `xe-0/0/1`, etc.) when applying this configuration. The command enables PIM sparse mode for IPv6 on the specified interface.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols pim interface <interface-name> family inet6 mode sparse'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ipv6 enable` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you need to specify both the interface and the logical unit (typically 0) where IPv6 should be enabled. This command enables IPv6 processing on the interface, similar to Cisco's `ipv6 enable`. You would need to apply this to each interface (ge-1/0/0, ge-1/0/1, ge-1/0/2 in this case) where IPv6 is required.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
